ef 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185680 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185680 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3472b7ea-9bc6-58cd-bf1a-0790d8d48e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185681 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185681 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185681 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6db8d629-42cc-5562-a4b8-39f1b9893e3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185682 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a6707d96-ec88-5b35-acfc-f90c52c72973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185683 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185683 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185683 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6cd7e58-adc6-5d97-83dc-0ef80f38597f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185684 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185684 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185684 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1fe0e570-a22a-5641-93a8-f6671eba3fcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185685 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185685 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185685 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2b2627b4-92aa-5fc7-b855-d9ad614c65ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185686 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185686 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185686 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "37a2400b-425f-50cd-9cf2-4636a9355424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185687 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185687 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185687 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61adcb0d-059f-5544-a8ef-924174a4c953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188004 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188004 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $\\theta \\in \\left[0, 2\\pi\\right)$. Given that\n\n$x'(\\theta) = 2\\cos2\\theta - 2\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 2\\cos\\theta+2\\sin2\\theta$,\nand $x'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right) = 0$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to 3\\pi/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$.\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "70e3ea0e-2f39-5715-98a3-2a0e686ffa76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188004 #edb/ref 1074 "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point $\\theta_0$, there is a horizontal tangent at $\\theta_0$ if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative $y'(x)$ takes the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nat this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that\n\n$$\nx'\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}2\\right)=0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}2$ into the expression for $y'(\\theta)$, we get\n$y^{′}(\\frac{3π}{2})|= 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}); = 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\sin (3π); = 0 + 0; = 0$.\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ using L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n$lim_(θ → 3π/2)\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → 3π/2)(y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{2\\cos θ + 2\\sin 2θ}{2\\cos 2θ - 2\\sin θ}); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin 2θ}{\\cos 2θ - \\sin θ}); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(((\\cos θ + \\sin 2θ)^{′})/((\\cos 2θ - \\sin θ)^{′})); = lim_(θ → 3π/2)(\\frac{-\\sin θ + 2\\cos 2θ}{-2\\sin 2θ - \\cos θ}); = (-\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}))/(-2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}) - \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2})); = (-\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) + 2\\cos (3π))/(-2\\sin (3π) - \\cos (\\frac{3π}{2})); = (1 + 2 \\cdot (-1))/(-2 \\cdot 0 - 0); = \\frac{1 - 2}{0}; = \\frac{-1}{0}; \\ne 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0$, there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$. In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/1b/1bff05165761ba775dfae73cd4d41c28f5a939b8caca602d516636a6fb05ae75.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note**: We'll discuss vertical tangents in more detail later in this lesson." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188023 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188023 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r=\\cot^2\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in \\left(0, \\pi\\right)$. Given that\n\n$x'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta(\\cos\\theta + 2\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta) = -\\cot\\theta\\csc\\theta(1+\\sin^2\\theta)$,\nand $y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $x'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right) = 0$\n2. $\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}=-\\infty$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n$\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ → 0^{+}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{-}; \\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ → 0^{-}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{+}$\n\n*It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188023 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2175ea86-60e7-5402-810d-1372b455c28d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188023 #edb/ref 1074 "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\theta_0\n$$\n\nif\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta_0)}{x'(\\theta_0)}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta_0) \\neq 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad x'(\\theta_0) = 0\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at $\\theta_0$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nat this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\theta_0^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that\n\n$$\ny'\\left(\\dfrac\\pi2\\right)=0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi2$ into the expression for $x'(\\theta)$, we get\n$x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2})|=-\\cot (\\frac{π}{2})(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cot (\\frac{π}{2})\\csc (\\frac{π}{2})); = 0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 1); = 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta\\to\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^+$, we obtain\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((-\\cot θ\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(-\\cot θ(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((-\\cotθ\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(-\\cotθ(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((\\csc θ(1 + \\sin^{2} θ))/(\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ)); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ})$.\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\csc θ + \\sin θ)|= \\csc (\\frac{π}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}); = 1 + 1; = 2$.\nTherefore, using the second hint, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ}); = \\frac{2}{0^{-}}; = - ∞$.\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part I, and the first hint, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})(\\frac{\\csc θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + 2\\cot θ\\csc θ}); = \\frac{2}{0^{+}}; = ∞$.\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2dc0665a0f979fd247d010a7b6d5891a758eff7e5357b881011ddf0d3f2f2002.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188297 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188316 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186188613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188316 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188316 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186188023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188316 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186189147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1045 "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186189116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188485 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e272f91b-9886-585a-977f-32ac3449f7d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188566 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188566 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188566 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r = 1 - \\sin θ$ for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) =-\\sin (θ) - \\cos (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = \\cos (θ) - \\sin (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → π/2)\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0$\n3. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $θ = π/2$\n\n*Hint: Use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188566 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46af20ff-a82f-5003-946a-59bcece07f0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188566 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188567 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188567 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188567 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the polar curve $r = \\sin θ - 1$ for $θ ∈ [0, 2π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) = \\sin (θ) + \\cos (2θ), y^{′}(θ) =-\\cos (θ) + \\sin (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(\\frac{π}{2}) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = \\frac{π}{2}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n$\\cos (θ) - 2\\sin (2θ) → 0^{-}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{-}; \\cos (θ) - 2\\sin (2θ) → 0^{+}asθ → (\\frac{π}{2})^{+}$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9cda14ea-b780-59bc-8a48-d618ff7d920f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188567 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1061 37500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c57d45c4-8a9c-599f-993b-2326fc1fb56f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189087 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1061 37995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f33de88b-4d60-5405-829e-a88dd1826520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189103 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1061 38153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ecb3fe0f-d538-57b4-b4e4-a660ce2892d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1061 38154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c1c429e-ced3-5909-92a2-519ae11a4322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189117 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1061 38606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f383aba-74ba-5583-9105-a9ee0b48e2fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189146 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189147 #edb/ref 1061 38614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189147 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9390067a-786b-5a47-889a-88feebe31790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189147 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the curve\n\n$$\nr = 1-\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/09/09b25bca42f62b6567cecf4b14987113003ccea6dd869a569be75af4fa1781c9.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve at this point.\n\n![](images/00/001914b359c9c95f6a8fceb655a5233242065eb4231765bcfc0d652ebbf4eed8.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines become steeper and steeper as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](images/40/40de3bbd7f8422ff4ab4af6091497579f8031b8a43296e466f8c23ed4a121097.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* as we approach $C$, it would appear that these tangent lines become vertical.\n\n![](images/ca/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes vertical like this, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at $C$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1065 6185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "69760698-2d63-55ca-aa44-895e4a5a7c66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189367 #edb/ref 1067 "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1064 "So, it seems that the curve\n\n$$\nr = 1-\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nhas a vertical tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nHowever, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad y'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → π/2)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= lim_(θ → π/2)((\\cos θ - \\sin 2θ)/(-\\sin θ - \\cos (2θ))) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π/2)((\\cos θ - \\sin (2θ))/(\\sin θ + \\cos (2θ)))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\ngives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nso evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx}|=-lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})((\\cos θ - \\sin (2θ)))/(\\sin θ + \\cos (2θ))); =-lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})(((\\cos θ - \\sin (2θ))^{′})/((\\sin θ + \\cos (2θ))^{′})); =-lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})((-\\sin θ - 2\\cos (2θ))/(\\cos θ - 2\\sin (2θ))); = lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})((\\sin θ + 2\\cos (2θ))/(\\cos θ - 2\\sin (2θ)))$.\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})(\\sin θ + 2\\cos (2θ))|= \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}); = \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cos (π); = 1 - 2; =-1$.\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n$\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-$,\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers. Therefore, we have\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{-})((\\sin θ + 2\\cos (2θ))/(\\cos θ - 2\\sin (2θ))); = \\frac{-1}{0^{-}}; = ∞$.\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n$lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})\\frac{dy}{dx}|= lim_(θ → (π/2)^{+})((\\sin θ + 2\\cos (2θ))/(\\cos θ - 2\\sin (2θ))); = \\frac{-1}{0^{+}}; = - ∞$.\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1065 6269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7b372073-8035-5855-9367-f29c35485805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189374 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Limits to Find Vertical Tangents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the curve\n\n$$\nr = 1+\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nshown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e72d6a7d4b490a37f99723d0cde12a378f038b43ba373547e9c8feaf71952717.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening at this point, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve.\n\n![](images/d0/d03ca27e8cecd32aebc0e6914ac2ab128bce57ab876b96e8aaa91b562041ba4c.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](images/4f/4fda95ed912c83ac36ec772b6afa6554ad8a81b7c98a330fd774d501b4bbecdf.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* of the tangent lines as we approach $C$, it would appear that they become horizontal.\n\n![](images/50/50f8bd688c55d8458a9db100469cd62138efc200402496d3adb18f90ea53e747.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes horizontal like this, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at $C$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1065 6303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9679f34-9ef1-55a0-ba1a-15cccaecb4a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189380 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1064 "So, it seems that the curve\n\n$$\nr = 1+\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nhas a horizontal tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad y'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → π)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= lim_(θ → π)((2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1)/(-\\sin θ(2\\cos θ + 1))) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)((2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1)/(\\sin θ(2\\cos θ + 1))) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1}{2\\sin θ\\cos θ + \\sin θ}) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1}{\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\ngives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nso evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(θ → π)\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1}{\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ}) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(((2\\cos^{2} θ + \\cos θ - 1)^{′})/((\\sin 2θ + \\sin θ)^{′})) \\\\\n&=-lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{-4\\cos θ\\sin θ - \\sin θ}{2\\cos 2θ + \\cos θ}) \\\\\n&= lim_(θ → π)(\\frac{4\\cos θ\\sin θ + \\sin θ}{2\\cos 2θ + \\cos θ}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\cos π\\sin π + \\sin π}{2\\cos 2π + \\cos π} \\\\\n&= (4 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (0) + 0)/(2 \\cdot 1 + (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0}{1} \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi\n$$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1065 6384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6a54c219-b6df-5e73-8673-14f48b1d69af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189391 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Limits to Find Horizontal Tangents" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
